Looking at the evidence, the worker rollback at t=580 (E15) stopped the worker restarts (E11, E12 show last restart at t=580, now 0.00/s). Processing rates have recovered across all services (E3-E9). However, 126 orphaned orders remain (E1, E10 shows queue_depth still at 359).

The key question: are these orphaned orders from the period when the worker was crashing (t=514–t=580), or are new orders still being orphaned? I need to check the age of the orphaned messages and whether the queue is draining properly now.

```json
{
  "investigate": [
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT COUNT(*) as cnt, MIN(created_at) as oldest, MAX(created_at) as newest, AVG(attempts) as avg_attempts FROM messages WHERE state NOT IN ('done','failed') AND available_at <= :now",
      "why": "Determine if orphaned/stuck messages are old (from the crash window t=514-580) or still being created, and whether they have been attempted"
    },
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT o.status, COUNT(*) as cnt FROM orders o LEFT JOIN messages m ON m.order_id = o.id AND m.state NOT IN ('done','failed') WHERE o.created_at >= 300 GROUP BY o.status ORDER BY cnt DESC",
      "why": "Check how many orders lack a live message (orphaned) vs those with active messages, to confirm requeue_orphaned_orders is the right repair"
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "service": "worker",
      "kind": "bad_deploy",
      "mechanism": "Worker v1.0.1 deployed at t=484 caused repeated health-check failures and restarts from t=514–t=580, preventing message processing and leaving 126+ orders without a live queue message.",
      "supporting": ["E1", "E10", "E11", "E12", "E14", "E15"],
      "refuting": [],
      "explains": ["I2_no_orphaned_orders"],
      "repair_plan": [
        {"action": "requeue_orphaned_orders", "params": {}}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Worker v1.0.1 (deployed t=484) crashed repeatedly until rollback at t=580, leaving 126 orders orphaned in the queue; the rollback fixed the crash but the orphaned orders still need to be requeued."
}
